# ADR-318 initial SLO qualification

Measured locally on 2026-08-23 with `scripts/run-slo-qualification.sh`.

Hardware: Linux 6.8.0-137-generic, amd64, 6 available processors, OpenJDK
21.0.11, 4,194,304,000-byte JVM maximum heap.

| ID | Measurement | Target | Result |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| NFR-001 | 10.226 ms render p95 | < 500 ms | Pass |
| NFR-002 | 3.776 ms detector p95 | < 1,000 ms | Pass |
| NFR-003 | 75.968 ms transaction p95 | < 100 ms | Pass |
| NFR-004 | 114.192 ms commit-service p95 | < 250 ms | Pass |
| NFR-005 | 44.424 commits/s; 100/100 rows | >= 2 commits/s | Pass |
| NFR-006 | 8/8 injected process-crash stages atomic | No partial commit | Pass |
| NFR-007 | 6/6 cross-novel HTTP cases denied | 100% | Pass |
| NFR-008 | 2.364 s RTO; integrity/replay passed | RPO 1 h, RTO 4 h | Pass |
| NFR-009 | 10/10 render-byte comparisons identical | Byte-identical | Pass |

NFR-005 also runs a controlled lock-contention test that verifies the
`sqlite_busy_total` metric. NFR-008 uses a newly encrypted live SQLite backup
and isolated restore; the measured RPO is zero seconds for the qualification
snapshot and no artifacts were missing.

The command writes complete machine-readable evidence to
`artifacts/slo/ADR-318.json`. It preserves each measured value, original target,
and pass/fail boolean, and exits nonzero when any criterion fails. Re-run on the
production hardware before launch; these numbers qualify only the hardware
declared above.
